## The endpoint count (commit 5)

An edge endpoint written by hand that is not shaped like an id becomes one shared `<lang>:<unknown>:0-0:<token>:external_symbol` node at finalize, and the validator that runs afterwards cannot tell which pass wrote it. Until now the rule was asserted only in 17 test files, for the 16 analyzers that had been repaired.

- `non_id_edge_endpoints(..., grammar_only=True)` asks whether each endpoint is an emitted symbol's id or parses as a five-slot id (ADR-0036 Ruling 1). It is called for every analyzer and every linker where the pass's own output is collected.
- New field `analysis_runs[].non_id_edge_endpoints`: an integer, or `null` for a pass that is not checked (the orchestrator's synthesis passes: seven on hypergumbo's own map built with the scip-python backend). When it is above zero, the run's `warnings` name the first three distinct endpoints.
- `SCHEMA_VERSION` 0.21.1 to 0.21.2 (an added field).
- Not counted, on purpose: a well-formed `0-0` stand-in whose last slot is a use-site word (`package`, `attribute`, `target`), which is ADR-0036 Ruling 2's unfinished migration and runs to thousands per repository.

Measured, base against this change, on a 10-language synthetic tree, dbus-broker, zstd and viper: nodes and edges identical, no stable id moved; the differences are `schema_version`, the new field, the warning line and two `pass_version` digests. Counts found: nix 6, latex 10, robot 2, v 4, gleam 4, odin 4, twig 2, meson 1, powershell 1 on the synthetic tree; meson 6 and rust 139 on dbus-broker; meson 6 and cpp 34 on zstd; 0 on viper. The rust and cpp endpoints have `::` in the name slot; finalize recovers a node for those, and they are counted because they are the same factory bypass. hypergumbo's own tree counts 44 (rust), so no validator check is added on top of the count in this PR. Cost: about 30 ms per run on zstd.

The analyzers themselves are not changed here; that work is tracked separately, and this count is its measure.

